From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Wed, 25 Nov 2026 10:40:44 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] fix(llama-cpp): a context canary the device refuses for
 memory is not a canary that failed

A model served with context on demand and the context canary - every
encoder here - creates a context for a request and computes the canary on
it before anything else. On a device that is full at that moment the
canary's graph is refused for memory. That refusal was answered "context
canary failed", counted as one and logged as a context that does not
reproduce the load's: a determinism failure that never happened. A caller
that acts on a canary failure - it stops trusting the run, and checks the
engine again from scratch - did so for a device that was full for a second.
At the load the same refusal left the model served with its contexts
unchecked for the life of the process.

The llama.cpp patch (0023) tells the two apart: llama_process returns
LLAMA_PROCESS_NO_MEMORY (-4) where the backend could not allocate memory
for the graph; the canary says so; at a request it is a context that
cannot be created ("the context cannot be created now ... try again
later", counted nowhere as a canary that failed), and at the load it fails
the load. A canary that was computed and differs is what it was.

Nothing changes for a request that is not refused, nor in what the backend
answers a request whose own computation fails.

Seen on the node, 2026-11-25: one context of an embedding model discarded
as a canary failure in the second the device filled at a start (thirteen
models preloading), one in 493 creations of that model and none since. The
engine's lines of that second were not kept, so that event is not proven
to be this; what a full device does is measured: on a CPU build with a
stand-in device that refuses graphs, before - 503 "context canary failed",
the counter at 1, and at the load the model served unchecked; after - 503
"the context cannot be created now", the counter at 0, the next request
served with the same vectors bit for bit, the load refused.

Assisted-by: Claude Code
---
 ...23-context-canary-refused-for-memory.patch | 230 ++++++++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 230 insertions(+)
 create mode 100644 backend/cpp/llama-cpp/patches/0023-context-canary-refused-for-memory.patch

diff --git a/backend/cpp/llama-cpp/patches/0023-context-canary-refused-for-memory.patch b/backend/cpp/llama-cpp/patches/0023-context-canary-refused-for-memory.patch
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..4c65bc3
--- /dev/null
+++ b/backend/cpp/llama-cpp/patches/0023-context-canary-refused-for-memory.patch
@@ -0,0 +1,230 @@
+server: a context canary the device refuses for memory is not a canary that failed
+
+With --context-on-demand and the canary on, a context created for a request
+computes the canary before anything else runs on it. On a device that is
+full at that moment the canary's graph is refused (GGML_STATUS_ALLOC_FAILED:
+a refused commit fails its graph, 0014 and 0018). canary_check answered
+MISMATCH for every canary it could not compute, so that refusal was
+answered "context canary failed", counted in context_canary_failures_total
+and logged as a context that "does not reproduce the canary of the load": a
+determinism failure that never happened. A caller that takes the engine at
+its word - it has no other - stops trusting the run.
+
+At the LOAD the same refusal took the other wrong turn: canary_record
+answered NONE, the model was served with "the contexts created on demand
+are not checked", and stayed unchecked for the life of the process.
+
+- llama_process / llama_encode / llama_decode return LLAMA_PROCESS_NO_MEMORY
+  (-4) for GGML_STATUS_ALLOC_FAILED. It was -2, the value of every other
+  failure before a graph is computed (the host's output buffer, the memory
+  module's): a caller could not tell a device full at that moment from
+  them. It is still < -1: a caller that only tests for a fatal error sees
+  no difference, and the server's own answer to a request ("Compute
+  error.") is unchanged.
+- the canary says COMMON_CANARY_NO_MEMORY when that is why it was not
+  computed.
+- at a request it is a context that cannot be created: the context is
+  discarded, the request is answered as that is ("the context cannot be
+  created now, the memory it needs may be in use: try again later", 503),
+  and nothing is counted as a canary that failed.
+- at the load it fails the load, which is tried again, rather than serve
+  contexts nothing checks.
+
+A canary that was computed and differs is what it was: "context canary
+failed", counted. A canary that could not be computed for any other reason
+is still a mismatch.
+
+Measured on a CPU build with a stand-in device that refuses graphs (a dev
+patch in no engine): before, the refusal of a context's warmup and canary
+was answered 503 "context canary failed" with the counter at 1, and a
+refusal at the load left the model served unchecked; after, 503 "the
+context cannot be created now", the counter at 0, the next request served
+with the same vectors bit for bit, and the load refused. The server's own
+context-on-demand tests pass (21).
+---
+diff --git a/common/common.cpp b/common/common.cpp
+index e6c5181..fa081a2 100644
+--- a/common/common.cpp
++++ b/common/common.cpp
+@@ -1467,8 +1467,10 @@ static const char * const COMMON_CANARY_TEXT       = "The quick brown fox jumps
+ static const size_t       COMMON_CANARY_MAX_TOKENS = 64;
+ 
+ // run the canary input on the context, the way the warmup run goes, and copy its output
+-// returns false if it cannot be computed
+-static bool common_context_canary_run(common_init_result & res, const common_params & params, std::vector<float> & out) {
++// returns false if it cannot be computed; no_memory: because the device had no memory for a graph of it
++static bool common_context_canary_run(common_init_result & res, const common_params & params, std::vector<float> & out, bool & no_memory) {
++    no_memory = false;
++
+     llama_model   * model = res.model();
+     llama_context * lctx  = res.context();
+ 
+@@ -1494,7 +1496,9 @@ static bool common_context_canary_run(common_init_result & res, const common_par
+     if (llama_model_has_encoder(model)) {
+         common_batch batch = common_batch_get_one(lctx, tokens);
+         all_outputs(batch);
+-        ok = llama_process(lctx, LLAMA_PROCESS_TYPE_ENCODE, batch.get()) == 0;
++        const int32_t ret = llama_process(lctx, LLAMA_PROCESS_TYPE_ENCODE, batch.get());
++        ok        = ret == 0;
++        no_memory = ret == LLAMA_PROCESS_NO_MEMORY;
+         if (llama_model_has_decoder(model)) {
+             llama_token decoder_start_token_id = llama_model_decoder_start_token(model);
+             if (decoder_start_token_id == LLAMA_TOKEN_NULL) {
+@@ -1508,7 +1512,9 @@ static bool common_context_canary_run(common_init_result & res, const common_par
+         all_outputs(batch);
+         std::vector<float> zeros;
+         common_batch_set_zero_state(batch, model, zeros);
+-        ok = llama_process(lctx, LLAMA_PROCESS_TYPE_DECODE, batch.get()) == 0;
++        const int32_t ret = llama_process(lctx, LLAMA_PROCESS_TYPE_DECODE, batch.get());
++        ok        = ret == 0;
++        no_memory = ret == LLAMA_PROCESS_NO_MEMORY;
+     }
+ 
+     if (ok) {
+@@ -1565,7 +1571,12 @@ common_canary_status common_init_result::canary_record(const common_params & par
+ 
+     std::vector<float> first;
+     std::vector<float> second;
+-    if (!common_context_canary_run(*this, params, first) || !common_context_canary_run(*this, params, second)) {
++    bool no_memory = false;
++    if (!common_context_canary_run(*this, params, first, no_memory) || !common_context_canary_run(*this, params, second, no_memory)) {
++        if (no_memory) {
++            COM_WRN("%s", "context canary: the device had no memory for its graph, it is not recorded\n");
++            return COMMON_CANARY_NO_MEMORY;
++        }
+         return COMMON_CANARY_NONE;
+     }
+     if (!common_context_canary_same(first, second)) {
+@@ -1588,7 +1599,12 @@ common_canary_status common_init_result::canary_check(const common_params & para
+     }
+ 
+     std::vector<float> now;
+-    if (!common_context_canary_run(*this, params, now)) {
++    bool no_memory = false;
++    if (!common_context_canary_run(*this, params, now, no_memory)) {
++        if (no_memory) {
++            COM_WRN("%s", "context canary: the device had no memory for its graph on this context\n");
++            return COMMON_CANARY_NO_MEMORY;
++        }
+         COM_WRN("%s", "context canary: its output could not be computed on this context\n");
+         return COMMON_CANARY_MISMATCH;
+     }
+diff --git a/common/common.h b/common/common.h
+index f3da483..0f97b98 100644
+--- a/common/common.h
++++ b/common/common.h
+@@ -1028,6 +1028,9 @@ enum common_canary_status {
+     COMMON_CANARY_OK,       // recorded, or reproduced
+     COMMON_CANARY_NONE,     // the output cannot be computed for this model: nothing is recorded, nothing is checked
+     COMMON_CANARY_MISMATCH, // the same input did not give the same values
++    // the device had no memory for the canary's graph (LLAMA_PROCESS_NO_MEMORY): nothing is known of the context's output,
++    // and nothing is wrong with it - a refusal of the moment, which a context created later may not meet
++    COMMON_CANARY_NO_MEMORY,
+ };
+ 
+ // note: defines the model, context, samplers, ets. lifetimes
+diff --git a/include/llama.h b/include/llama.h
+index e4ccb13..ac3488e 100644
+--- a/include/llama.h
++++ b/include/llama.h
+@@ -652,6 +652,12 @@ extern "C" {
+     // Returns the total size of all the tensors in the model in bytes
+     LLAMA_API uint64_t llama_model_size(const struct llama_model * model);
+ 
++    // llama_process / llama_encode / llama_decode: the backend could not allocate memory for the graph - its
++    // buffers at allocation, or the device's own refusal while it computed (GGML_STATUS_ALLOC_FAILED). It had the
++    // value -2 of every other failure before a graph is computed (the output buffer of the host, the memory
++    // module's own): a caller could not tell a device full at that moment from them.
++    #define LLAMA_PROCESS_NO_MEMORY (-4)
++
+     // Parking: the weights leave the device and stay in host memory, the model and everything that refers to its
+     // tensors stay valid; unparking copies them back. A model may be parked only while NO context of it exists, and
+     // a context can be created again only after it is unparked.
+@@ -1014,6 +1020,8 @@ extern "C" {
+     //    2 - aborted     (processed ubatches will remain in the context's memory)
+     //   -1 - invalid input batch
+     // < -1 - fatal error (processed ubatches will remain in the context's memory)
++    //   -4 - among them: the backend could not allocate memory for the graph (LLAMA_PROCESS_NO_MEMORY). The
++    //        device was full at that moment; nothing is wrong with the batch, and the same call may succeed later
+     DEPRECATED(LLAMA_API int32_t llama_decode(
+             struct llama_context * ctx,
+               struct llama_batch   batch),
+diff --git a/src/llama-context.cpp b/src/llama-context.cpp
+index 8626e12..ff59ae6 100644
+--- a/src/llama-context.cpp
++++ b/src/llama-context.cpp
+@@ -1985,7 +1985,7 @@ int llama_context::encode(const llama_batch_ext & batch_inp) {
+     if (!res) {
+         switch (status) {
+             case GGML_STATUS_ABORTED:      return  2;
+-            case GGML_STATUS_ALLOC_FAILED: return -2;
++            case GGML_STATUS_ALLOC_FAILED: return LLAMA_PROCESS_NO_MEMORY;
+             case GGML_STATUS_FAILED:       return -3;
+             case GGML_STATUS_SUCCESS:      GGML_ABORT("should not happen");
+         }
+@@ -2404,7 +2404,7 @@ int llama_context::decode(const llama_batch_ext & batch_inp) {
+ 
+             switch (status) {
+                 case GGML_STATUS_ABORTED:      return  2;
+-                case GGML_STATUS_ALLOC_FAILED: return -2;
++                case GGML_STATUS_ALLOC_FAILED: return LLAMA_PROCESS_NO_MEMORY;
+                 case GGML_STATUS_FAILED:       return -3;
+                 case GGML_STATUS_SUCCESS:      GGML_ABORT("should not happen");
+             }
+diff --git a/tools/server/README.md b/tools/server/README.md
+index b054b6f..96105f3 100644
+--- a/tools/server/README.md
++++ b/tools/server/README.md
+@@ -2632,7 +2632,7 @@ Notes:
+ - The context is created with the parameters of the first load: the same size, batch sizes and device placement, without fitting them again to the memory that is free at that moment.
+ - Not supported yet together with speculative decoding or a multimodal projector: the server refuses to start.
+ 
+-**The context canary.** A context created on demand is new memory and a new first pass. Unless `--no-context-canary` is given, the server records at the load the output its context gives for a fixed short input - the pooled embedding with `--embedding`, the logits of the last token otherwise - computed twice, and it refuses to start if the two differ. Every context created afterwards computes the same input before any request runs on it: one that does not give the same values, bit for bit, is discarded, and the request it was created for is answered with error `503` (`unavailable_error`, its message starting with `context canary failed`) and can be retried. It costs one small pass per creation. After `POST /lora-adapters` the next context created records it again. Switch it off for a model whose backend does not reproduce its own output bit for bit.
++**The context canary.** A context created on demand is new memory and a new first pass. Unless `--no-context-canary` is given, the server records at the load the output its context gives for a fixed short input - the pooled embedding with `--embedding`, the logits of the last token otherwise - computed twice, and it refuses to start if the two differ. Every context created afterwards computes the same input before any request runs on it: one that does not give the same values, bit for bit, is discarded, and the request it was created for is answered with error `503` (`unavailable_error`, its message starting with `context canary failed`) and can be retried. A canary the device had no memory to compute is not one that failed: nothing is known of that context, and it is the refusal of a context that cannot be created (`503`, `the context cannot be created now ...`), counted in no canary metric; at the load it fails the load, rather than serve contexts that are not checked. It costs one small pass per creation. After `POST /lora-adapters` the next context created records it again. Switch it off for a model whose backend does not reproduce its own output bit for bit.
+ 
+ `GET /metrics` reports `llamacpp:context_loaded` (1 while the context exists), `llamacpp:context_creations_total`, `llamacpp:context_creation_seconds_total` (the canary's pass included), `llamacpp:context_canary` (1 while the contexts created are checked) and `llamacpp:context_canary_failures_total`.
+ 
+diff --git a/tools/server/server-context.cpp b/tools/server/server-context.cpp
+index ce8e3fc..6d509f1 100644
+--- a/tools/server/server-context.cpp
++++ b/tools/server/server-context.cpp
+@@ -1329,12 +1329,24 @@ private:
+         if (metrics.ctx_canary) {
+             // before any task runs on it: a context that does not reproduce the canary of the load is not used
+             bool same;
++            common_canary_status canary;
+             if (ctx_canary_stale) {
+                 // the lora adapters were set since the canary was recorded: this context records it again
+-                same = llama_init->canary_record(params_base) == COMMON_CANARY_OK;
+-                ctx_canary_stale = !same;
++                canary = llama_init->canary_record(params_base);
++                ctx_canary_stale = canary != COMMON_CANARY_OK;
+             } else {
+-                same = llama_init->canary_check(params_base) == COMMON_CANARY_OK;
++                canary = llama_init->canary_check(params_base);
++            }
++            same = canary == COMMON_CANARY_OK;
++            if (canary == COMMON_CANARY_NO_MEMORY) {
++                // the canary was not computed: the device refused its graph. Nothing says this context computes wrong -
++                // it is the refusal of a context that cannot be created, and is not counted as a canary that failed
++                llama_init->free_context();
++                SRV_WRN("%s", "the device had no memory for the canary of the context created, it is discarded\n");
++                if (was_parked) {
++                    weights_park(); // a request that is refused leaves the device as it found it
++                }
++                return CONTEXT_NOT_CREATED;
+             }
+             if (ctx_canary_fail > 0) {
+                 ctx_canary_fail--;
+@@ -1659,6 +1671,11 @@ private:
+                     SRV_ERR("%s", "the model does not give the same output twice for the same input: the context canary cannot "
+                                   "be recorded. start with --no-context-canary to serve it without the check\n");
+                     return false;
++                case COMMON_CANARY_NO_MEMORY:
++                    // not served unchecked for the life of the process because of one refusal: the load fails, and is tried again
++                    SRV_ERR("%s", "the device had no memory for the context canary: the load fails rather than serve "
++                                  "contexts that are not checked\n");
++                    return false;
+             }
+         }
+ 
